insert into d (name, yr, txt) values
  ('Battle of Kadesh', -1274, 'Around 1274 BC the Egyptian pharaoh Ramesses II fought the Hittite king Muwatalli II near Kadesh on the Orontes River, in what is now Syria. It was among the largest chariot battles ever fought, and Ramesses was nearly trapped by a Hittite ambush before his forces rallied. Both sides claimed victory, and some fifteen years later they signed one of history''s earliest surviving peace treaties, a copy of which hangs at the United Nations headquarters.'),
  ('Battle of Kadesh (Egyptian-Levantine theatre)', -1274, 'At Kadesh on the Orontes, Ramesses II led about 20,000 Egyptians against a Hittite army that had hidden behind the city and then sprang a chariot attack on one of his divisions. The young pharaoh''s own counterattack saved the day, and he celebrated the result as a great victory on temple walls across Egypt, though the Hittites kept Kadesh and Syria. It is the earliest battle whose tactics and numbers are recorded in detail.'),
  ('Battle of Muye', -1046, 'At Muye, around 1046 BC, King Wu of Zhou defeated the last Shang king, Di Xin, and captured the Shang capital Yin. According to tradition, many Shang soldiers were unwilling to fight and turned against their own king, who died in the burning of his palace. The victory founded the Zhou dynasty, which lasted about 800 years, and made the battle a textbook example of the Mandate of Heaven, the idea that Heaven gives and withdraws the right to rule.'),
  ('Zhou conquest of Shang', -1046, 'In about 1046 BC the Zhou, a vassal state in the west, overthrew the Shang dynasty after King Wu''s victory at Muye. The Zhou justified the conquest with the Mandate of Heaven, claiming that the last Shang ruler had lost his right to rule through cruelty and misrule. Zhou rule lasted roughly eight centuries, longer than any other Chinese dynasty, and the idea of the Mandate shaped Chinese political thought for the next three thousand years.'),
  ('Battle of Qarqar', -853, 'In 853 BC the Assyrian king Shalmaneser III fought a coalition of about a dozen kings, led by Hadadezer of Damascus and including Ahab of Israel, at Qarqar in Syria. The Assyrians claimed a great victory, yet they did not advance further for several years, which suggests the allies held them off. The battle is also notable because an Assyrian inscription names Ahab, one of the earliest mentions of an Israelite king outside the Bible.'),
  ('First Olympic Games (traditional date)', -776, 'Tradition dates the first ancient Olympic Games to 776 BC at Olympia, in honour of Zeus, and records Coroebus of Elis as the winner of the footrace and the first Olympic champion. The games grew into a festival that all Greek city-states attended, with a sacred truce so that athletes could travel safely. Greeks later counted time in four-year Olympiads, and the games lasted nearly twelve centuries until they were ended in the late 4th century AD.'),
  ('Battle of Changshao', -684, 'In 684 BC the small state of Lu beat the much larger Qi at Changshao in the Shandong region, thanks to its adviser Cao Gui. He had the Lu army wait while the Qi drums sounded three times, and counterattacked only when the enemy''s spirit had faded. The account in the Zuo Zhuan is still quoted in Chinese writing on warfare as a lesson in timing and morale.'),
  ('Battle of Bi', -597, 'In 597 BC the army of King Zhuang of Chu defeated the state of Jin at Bi, in what is now Henan. The battle came about three and a half decades after Jin had beaten Chu at Chengpu, and the Chu victory confirmed Zhuang as the leading hegemon among the states of the Zhou world. It shows how the Spring and Autumn period was shaped by the rivalry between the powerful southern state of Chu and the northern states led by Jin.'),
  ('Fall of Nineveh', -612, 'In 612 BC an army of Babylonians and Medes besieged and destroyed Nineveh, the Assyrian capital and one of the greatest cities in the world. The Assyrian king Sinsharishkun probably died during the fall of the city. The destruction ended the Neo-Assyrian Empire, which had dominated the Near East for centuries, and left Babylon and the Medes as the new powers in the region.'),
  ('Battle of Yanling', -575, 'In 575 BC the states of Jin and Chu met at Yanling during the Spring and Autumn period. Jin won, and King Gong of Chu lost an eye to an arrow in the fighting. The victory checked Chu''s expansion to the north for a time, but Jin was already torn by quarrels among its great noble families, an early sign of the breakdown that later split the state in three.'),
  ('Confucius born', -551, 'Confucius was born in about 551 BC in the state of Lu, in present-day Shandong. He became a teacher and adviser whose sayings, collected by his followers in the Analects, stressed moral conduct, respect for family and elders, and virtuous government. Confucianism became the foundation of Chinese education and government for more than two thousand years and spread across East Asia.'),
  ('Battle of Opis', -539, 'In September 539 BC Cyrus the Great''s Persian army defeated the Babylonian army of Nabonidus at Opis on the Tigris, north of Babylon. The victory opened the road to the capital, and Babylon fell to the Persians a few days later with little fighting. Opis was the last major battle between the Achaemenid and Neo-Babylonian empires and ended Babylonian independence.'),
  ('Cyrus conquers Babylon', -539, 'In October 539 BC the Persian army of Cyrus the Great took Babylon, ending the Neo-Babylonian Empire and the last native dynasty of Mesopotamia. Cyrus''s own cylinder inscription presents him as a liberator who respected local gods and let deported peoples return home, among them the Jews exiled in Babylon. The conquest made Persia the greatest empire the world had yet seen.'),
  ('Persian Wars', -499, 'From 499 to 449 BC the Greek city-states fought the mighty Persian Empire in a series of wars that included the famous battles of Marathon, Thermopylae, Salamis and Plataea. The Greeks, far smaller and often divided, turned back two Persian invasions and kept their independence. The victory shaped the rise of Athens and the flowering of classical Greek culture and politics.'),
  ('Persian Wars begin', -499, 'In 499 BC the Greek cities of Ionia on the coast of Asia Minor rose in revolt against Persian rule, and Athens and Eretria sent ships to help; the rebels even burned the Persian regional capital of Sardis. The revolt was crushed by 493 BC, but King Darius I resolved to punish Athens and Eretria, which led to the Persian invasions of Greece. A local rebellion thus started the conflict that decided whether Greece would remain independent.'),
  ('Battle of Marathon', -490, 'In 490 BC about 10,000 Athenians and Plataeans defeated a larger Persian force at Marathon, on the coast northeast of Athens. The Athenian general Miltiades strengthened his flanks to envelop the Persians, who fled to their ships. It was the first great Greek victory over Persia, and the legend of a runner carrying the news to Athens inspired the modern marathon race.'),
  ('Battle of Salamis', -480, 'In September 480 BC a smaller Greek fleet under the Athenian Themistocles lured the Persian navy of Xerxes into the narrow straits off the island of Salamis and destroyed much of it. Crowded in the channel, the Persian ships could not use their numbers. The victory saved Greece from conquest by sea, forced Xerxes to withdraw with part of his army, and made Athens the leading naval power in the Greek world.'),
  ('Battle of Thermopylae', -480, 'In 480 BC a small Greek force led by the Spartan king Leonidas held the narrow pass of Thermopylae against Xerxes'' vast Persian army for three days. When a local traitor showed the Persians a path around the pass, Leonidas sent most of the Greeks away and stayed with about 300 Spartans and some others to fight to the death. The delay gave the Greek fleet and cities time to prepare, and the stand became a lasting symbol of courage against overwhelming odds.'),
  ('Battle of Plataea', -479, 'In 479 BC a Greek army led by Sparta decisively defeated the Persian army of Mardonius at Plataea in Boeotia after weeks of maneuvering. Mardonius was killed and the Persian camp was stormed. It was the final land battle of the Persian invasion and, together with the naval victory at Mycale reportedly on the same day, ended Persia''s attempt to conquer mainland Greece.'),
  ('Peloponnesian War', -431, 'From 431 to 404 BC Athens and its empire fought Sparta and its allies in the Peloponnesian War, a struggle for supremacy over Greece. The plague in Athens, the disastrous Sicilian expedition and Persian money for the Spartan fleet turned the war against Athens, which finally surrendered in 404 BC. The war ended the golden age of Athens and left Sparta dominant, and Thucydides'' history of it still shapes how we think about power politics.'),
  ('Battle of Delium', -424, 'In 424 BC an Athenian army was beaten by the Boeotians, allies of Sparta, near the sanctuary of Delium on the Boeotian border during the Peloponnesian War. Athens lost about a thousand heavy infantry, and the sudden arrival of Boeotian cavalry from behind a hill decided the battle. Famous names took part: the philosopher Socrates fought as a hoplite and, according to Plato, retreated bravely.'),
  ('Battle of Amphipolis', -422, 'In 422 BC the Spartan general Brasidas and the Athenian commander Cleon fought outside Amphipolis in Thrace, and both were killed, though the Spartans won. Brasidas had captured Amphipolis from Athens two years earlier, and Athens wanted it back. With the two leading war hawks dead, both sides accepted the Peace of Nicias in 421 BC.'),
  ('Battle of Mantinea (418 BC)', -418, 'In 418 BC Sparta defeated an alliance of Argos, Mantinea and Athens at Mantinea, the largest land battle of the Peloponnesian War. The Spartan king Agis II, who had been criticized for an earlier failed campaign, won it with a disciplined advance. The victory restored Sparta''s prestige as the leading land power in Greece and ended the plan of Athens'' allies to build a rival bloc in the Peloponnese.'),
  ('Trial and death of Socrates', -399, 'In 399 BC an Athenian jury convicted the philosopher Socrates of impiety and of corrupting the young and sentenced him to death. He refused to flee and drank poison hemlock, as Plato describes in the Phaedo. His death made him a martyr for free inquiry, and Plato''s writings about him became a foundation of Western philosophy.'),
  ('Battle of Nemea (394 BC)', -394, 'In 394 BC a Spartan army defeated the combined forces of Athens, Thebes, Corinth and Argos on the dry bed of the Nemea River near Corinth. It was a decisive Spartan victory, and together with the battle of Coronea later that year it gave Sparta the upper hand in the early fighting of the Corinthian War. The war was fought because Sparta''s rivals resented its dominance in Greece after the Peloponnesian War.'),
  ('Alexander conquers Persia', -334, 'In 334 BC Alexander the Great, aged 21, crossed the Hellespont into Asia with about 40,000 men to attack the Persian Empire. In a decade of campaigns he defeated Darius III in three great battles and overthrew the Achaemenid dynasty, reaching as far east as the Indus. His conquests spread Greek language and culture across the Near East and created the Hellenistic world.'),
  ('Alexander the Great''s conquests', -334, 'Between 334 and 323 BC Alexander of Macedon conquered the Persian Empire and marched through Egypt, Mesopotamia, Persia and Central Asia to the Indus Valley without losing a major battle. He died in Babylon at 32, and his generals divided his empire among themselves. The Hellenistic kingdoms that grew from it carried Greek language and ideas from Egypt to Afghanistan for about three centuries.'),
  ('Battle of the Granicus', -334, 'In May 334 BC Alexander the Great won the first of his three great battles against Persia by forcing the crossing of the Granicus River in northwestern Asia Minor against the army of the Persian satraps. He led the cavalry charge himself and was nearly killed until his companion Cleitus saved him. The victory opened western Asia Minor to the Macedonians, and the Greek mercenaries who fought for Persia were mostly killed or enslaved.'),
  ('Battle of Issus', -333, 'In November 333 BC Alexander defeated the Persian king Darius III at Issus in southern Anatolia, on a narrow plain between mountains and the sea where the Persians could not use their numbers. Darius fled, leaving his family behind, and Alexander treated them with respect. The victory opened Syria and Egypt to Alexander and marked the point at which the Persian Empire began to fall apart.'),
  ('Siege of Gaza', -332, 'In autumn 332 BC Alexander besieged Gaza, a heavily fortified city on a mound on the road to Egypt, and took it on his third assault with siege engines and earthworks. He was wounded in the shoulder during the siege. Gaza''s fall opened the way to Egypt, which welcomed him as a liberator from Persian rule.'),
  ('Siege of Tyre', -332, 'In 332 BC Alexander took the island city of Tyre after a seven-month siege by building a causeway across the sea to its walls and bringing a fleet to attack it. It was one of the greatest siege feats of the ancient world. Alexander''s causeway still joins the former island to the Lebanese coast today.'),
  ('Battle of Gaugamela', -331, 'On 1 October 331 BC Alexander the Great defeated the much larger army of Darius III on an open plain near Gaugamela in northern Mesopotamia. Using a flexible formation, he led a cavalry charge at the gap that opened in the Persian line and drove Darius from the field. It was the final great battle between the two kings and the death blow to the Achaemenid Empire, whose heartlands of Babylon, Susa and Persepolis fell to Alexander soon after.'),
  ('Battle of Persian Gate', -330, 'In the winter of 330 BC the Persian satrap Ariobarzanes held the mountain pass known as the Persian Gate against Alexander''s army for about a month, blocking the road to Persepolis. Alexander found a path around the pass, probably with the help of a local shepherd, and attacked from behind. The last stand did not save the Persian capital, which Alexander captured and burned soon afterwards.'),
  ('Battle of Hydaspes', -326, 'In May 326 BC Alexander the Great defeated the Indian king Porus on the banks of the Hydaspes River in the Punjab. He crossed the river secretly upstream during a thunderstorm and attacked Porus''s army, including its war elephants, from the flank. Porus''s courage impressed Alexander so much that he let him keep his kingdom; it was Alexander''s last great battle, and soon afterwards his exhausted army refused to march any further east.'),
  ('Seleucid Empire established', -312, 'In 312 BC Seleucus I Nicator won back Babylon, and the date became the start of the Seleucid era and of the Seleucid Empire, one of the successor states to Alexander''s empire. At its height it stretched from the Aegean coast of Anatolia to the borders of India, ruled by a Greek-Macedonian elite over many peoples. It survived until 63 BC, when the Roman general Pompey annexed what was left of it as the province of Syria.'),
  ('Ptolemaic rule in Egypt', -305, 'In 305 BC Ptolemy I Soter, one of Alexander''s generals, declared himself king of Egypt and founded the Ptolemaic dynasty. His family ruled from Alexandria for 275 years, the last dynasty of ancient Egypt, and made the city a great centre of learning with its Library and Lighthouse. It ended in 30 BC with the death of Cleopatra VII, after which Egypt became a Roman province.'),
  ('Battle of Sentinum', -295, 'In 295 BC the Romans defeated a formidable coalition of Samnites, Etruscans, Umbrians and Gauls at Sentinum in the Marches region of Italy, in the decisive battle of the Third Samnite War. The Roman consul Publius Decius Mus is said to have deliberately sacrificed his own life to win the gods'' favour for Rome. The victory broke up the alliance and paved the way for Roman mastery of central Italy.'),
  ('Battle of Heraclea', -280, 'In 280 BC King Pyrrhus of Epirus, who had come to Italy to help the Greek city of Tarentum, defeated a Roman army at Heraclea with the help of war elephants, which the Romans had never faced. The victory was costly, since he lost many of his best troops. It was the first of the costly victories that gave us the phrase ''Pyrrhic victory''.'),
  ('Battle of Asculum', -279, 'In 279 BC Pyrrhus of Epirus defeated the Romans again at Asculum in southern Italy, but at such a cost that he is said to have remarked that another such victory would ruin him. Rome could replace its losses, while Pyrrhus, far from home, could not. The battle gave the language the phrase ''Pyrrhic victory'', and Pyrrhus eventually left Italy.'),
  ('First Punic War', -264, 'From 264 to 241 BC Rome and Carthage fought over Sicily in the longest continuous war and greatest naval war of antiquity. Rome, which had no navy at the start, built fleets and learned to win at sea, finally destroying the Carthaginian fleet off the Aegates Islands. Rome gained Sicily, its first overseas province, and began to emerge as a Mediterranean power, while the harsh peace helped lead to the Second Punic War.'),
  ('Battle of Changping', -260, 'In 260 BC the state of Qin defeated Zhao at Changping after a two-year stalemate, in the biggest battle of China''s Warring States period. According to the historian Sima Qian, the Qin commander Bai Qi had the surrendered Zhao soldiers killed, a figure of hundreds of thousands that modern historians doubt. The victory broke the power of Qin''s strongest rival and cleared the path for Qin to unify China about forty years later.'),
  ('Battle of Carthage (First Punic War)', -256, 'In 256 BC, after winning the great naval battle off Cape Ecnomus, the Roman consul Marcus Atilius Regulus landed an army in Africa and threatened Carthage itself. The next year the Carthaginians, led by the Spartan mercenary Xanthippus, crushed the Romans in open country and captured Regulus. The failed invasion showed how risky overseas campaigns were and helped drag the First Punic War on for another fourteen years.'),
  ('Battle of Telamon', -225, 'In 225 BC a Roman army caught between two forces defeated a huge Celtic invasion at Telamon on the coast of Etruria. The Celts, led by the Gaesatae kings Concolitanus and Aneroestes, were trapped between the armies of the two consuls, and one consul, Regulus, was killed. The victory removed the Celtic threat to Rome and allowed Roman power to extend into northern Italy.'),
  ('Qin unifies China', -221, 'In 221 BC King Zheng of Qin completed his conquest of the rival Warring States and unified China for the first time, taking the title Qin Shi Huangdi, the ''First Emperor''. He standardized writing, weights, measures and currency, joined northern walls into what became the Great Wall, and was buried with the Terracotta Army. The Qin dynasty itself collapsed within fifteen years, but the empire it created became the model for later Chinese states.'),
  ('Battle of Trebia', -218, 'In December 218 BC Hannibal destroyed a Roman army at the Trebia River in northern Italy, in the first major battle of the Second Punic War. He lured the Romans across the icy river on a cold morning and ambushed them with a hidden force. The victory brought the Gallic tribes over to Hannibal''s side and began his string of victories in Italy.'),
  ('Second Punic War', -218, 'In 218 BC the Carthaginian general Hannibal crossed the Alps with elephants and invaded Italy, where he crushed Roman armies at Trebia, Lake Trasimene and Cannae. Rome refused to give in, and the general Scipio eventually carried the war to Africa and defeated Hannibal at Zama in 202 BC. Carthage was left a minor power, and Rome became the dominant power of the western Mediterranean.'),
  ('Battle of Lake Trasimene', -217, 'In June 217 BC Hannibal ambushed a Roman army under Gaius Flaminius on the shore of Lake Trasimene in Italy, using morning mist and the steep hills to trap it against the water. Flaminius was killed and most of his army destroyed. It is remembered as the largest ambush in military history.'),
  ('Battle of Raphia', -217, 'In June 217 BC at Raphia, on the edge of the Sinai, Ptolemy IV of Egypt defeated the Seleucid king Antiochus III in one of the largest battles of the Hellenistic world, with elephants on both sides. A large part of Ptolemy''s army was made up of native Egyptians trained to fight as phalanx soldiers. The victory kept Coele-Syria, the land between Egypt and Syria, in Ptolemaic hands, but arming the Egyptians later fuelled unrest in Egypt.'),
  ('Battle of Cannae (North African Carthaginian army)', -216, 'On 2 August 216 BC Hannibal surrounded and almost annihilated a much larger Roman army at Cannae in southern Italy, using a double envelopment in which his centre gave ground while his cavalry closed around the Romans. Tens of thousands of Romans died in a single day, one of the bloodiest days in military history. Cannae became the model of a perfect battle of encirclement and has been studied by military planners for over two thousand years.'),
  ('Battle of Julu', -207, 'In 207 BC the rebel general Xiang Yu of Chu crossed the Zhang River, smashed his cooking pots and sank his boats to commit his men to victory, and defeated the bigger Qin army of Zhang Han at Julu. The main army defending the Qin dynasty was destroyed, and its commander later surrendered. The battle sealed the fate of the dynasty and made Xiang Yu the most powerful warlord in China for a time.'),
  ('Battle of the Metaurus', -207, 'In 207 BC two Roman consuls, Claudius Nero and Livius Salinator, destroyed the army of Hannibal''s brother Hasdrubal at the Metaurus River in Italy as it marched to join him. Nero had secretly hurried north with part of his army to reinforce his colleague, a daring move that surprised Hasdrubal. Hasdrubal died in the battle and his head was thrown into Hannibal''s camp; Hannibal never received the reinforcements he needed to win the war.'),
  ('Battle of Gaixia', -202, 'In the winter of 203-202 BC the forces of Liu Bang surrounded the army of Xiang Yu, king of Western Chu, at Gaixia. According to tradition, the Han troops sang songs of Chu at night to make the besieged believe their homeland had fallen; Xiang Yu broke out but later killed himself at the Wu River. His death ended the civil war that followed the fall of Qin, and Liu Bang became the first emperor of the Han dynasty.'),
  ('Battle of Zama', -202, 'In 202 BC the Roman general Scipio Africanus defeated Hannibal at Zama, near Carthage, ending the Second Punic War. Scipio''s cavalry, helped by the Numidian horsemen of Masinissa, returned at the decisive moment to hit Hannibal''s army in the rear. Carthage had to give up its navy, its overseas empire and large sums of money, and Hannibal went into exile; Rome was now the dominant power of the western Mediterranean.'),
  ('Han dynasty founded', -202, 'In 202 BC Liu Bang, a peasant who had led a rebellion against the Qin dynasty, defeated his rival Xiang Yu and became Emperor Gaozu, founder of the Han dynasty. The Han lasted about four centuries, made Confucian ideas the basis of government, expanded the empire into Central Asia and opened the Silk Road. Even today the majority ethnic group of China calls itself the Han.'),
  ('Battle of Cynoscephalae', -197, 'In June 197 BC a Roman army under Titus Quinctius Flamininus defeated King Philip V of Macedon at Cynoscephalae in Thessaly, in a battle that neither side had planned among hills and fog. The more flexible Roman legion beat the rigid Macedonian phalanx on uneven ground. The victory ended the Second Macedonian War and showed the legion''s superiority over the phalanx, and Flamininus then proclaimed the Greek cities free.'),
  ('Battle of Magnesia', -190, 'In late 190 BC or early 189 BC the Romans under the consul Lucius Cornelius Scipio, with the army of their ally Pergamon, defeated King Antiochus III of the Seleucid Empire at Magnesia in western Asia Minor. The Seleucid scythed chariots and elephants were thrown into confusion by Roman missile troops, and the army collapsed. The Treaty of Apamea then forced Antiochus out of most of Asia Minor and made Rome the dominant power in the eastern Mediterranean.'),
  ('Destruction of Carthage', -146, 'In 146 BC Roman troops under Scipio Aemilianus stormed Carthage after a siege of almost three years, and the city was burned for days. Most of its people were killed or enslaved, and the site was cursed (the story that the soil was salted is a later legend). The destruction ended the Third Punic War and removed Rome''s last rival in the western Mediterranean.'),
  ('Roman conquest of Greece', -146, 'In 146 BC the Roman consul Lucius Mummius defeated the Achaean League and destroyed the rich city of Corinth, ending the independence of mainland Greece. Greece became part of the Roman sphere, and its art and philosophy were carried off to Rome and eagerly copied. As the poet Horace later wrote, captive Greece took her fierce conqueror captive, since Greek culture shaped Roman life.'),
  ('Battle of Arausio', -105, 'On 6 October 105 BC two Roman armies, whose commanders Caepio and Maximus quarrelled and would not cooperate, were destroyed by the Cimbri and Teutones at Arausio on the Rhône. It was Rome''s worst defeat since Cannae, with a huge number of soldiers killed. The disaster caused panic in Rome and led to the army reforms of Gaius Marius, who was elected consul again and again to meet the threat.'),
  ('Siege of Athens and Piraeus', -87, 'In 87-86 BC the Roman general Sulla besieged Athens and its port Piraeus, which had sided with King Mithridates VI of Pontus in the First Mithridatic War. Sulla cut down sacred groves for siege timber and took Athens in March 86 BC, and his troops plundered it brutally. It was a low point for the city that had been the intellectual capital of Greece, and it opened the way for Sulla''s victories over Mithridates'' armies in Greece.'),
  ('Spartacus revolt', -73, 'In 73 BC the gladiator Spartacus escaped from a training school at Capua with about 70 others and gathered tens of thousands of slaves and poor people into an army that beat several Roman forces. After two years of fighting in Italy, Marcus Licinius Crassus crushed the rebels and crucified about 6,000 captives along the Appian Way. It was the only slave revolt that directly threatened the Roman heartland, and Spartacus became a lasting symbol of resistance to oppression.'),
  ('Siege of Jerusalem', -63, 'In 63 BC the Roman general Pompey, called in to settle a dispute between two Hasmonean brothers, besieged Jerusalem for three months and stormed the Temple Mount. Thousands of Jews were killed, and Pompey entered the Holy of Holies, which shocked the Jewish people. The conquest ended the independent Jewish state and made Judea a client state of Rome, which set the stage for centuries of Roman control.'),
  ('Battle of Carrhae', -53, 'In 53 BC a Roman invasion force of seven legions under Marcus Licinius Crassus was lured into the Mesopotamian desert and destroyed near Carrhae by Parthian horse archers and armoured cataphracts under the general Surena. Crassus was killed during a parley, and thousands of Romans were captured, along with several legionary eagles. It was one of Rome''s worst defeats and began centuries of rivalry between Rome and Parthia.'),
  ('Caesar crosses the Rubicon', -49, 'In January 49 BC Julius Caesar led his legion across the Rubicon, the small river marking the border of his province, into Italy, defying the Senate''s order to disband his army. By tradition he said ''the die is cast''. The act began a civil war against Pompey that made Caesar master of the Roman world, and ''crossing the Rubicon'' still means passing a point of no return.'),
  ('Battle of Pharsalus', -48, 'On 9 August 48 BC Julius Caesar defeated Pompey''s much larger army at Pharsalus in Greece, in the decisive battle of their civil war. Pompey''s cavalry was beaten by a hidden reserve line of Caesar''s infantry, and his army collapsed. Pompey fled to Egypt, where he was murdered, and Caesar emerged as the undisputed master of Rome.'),
  ('Battle of Thapsus', -46, 'On 6 April 46 BC Julius Caesar defeated the Optimates under Metellus Scipio and their ally King Juba of Numidia at Thapsus in North Africa. The defeat was followed by the suicides of several Republican leaders, among them Cato the Younger, the Republic''s most stubborn defender. The victory ended organized senatorial resistance in Africa and strengthened Caesar''s grip as dictator.'),
  ('Battle of Utica', -46, 'After Caesar''s victory at Thapsus in 46 BC, the Republican leader Cato the Younger held the city of Utica in North Africa. Rather than surrender to Caesar, he arranged for the safety of Utica''s people and the soldiers, then killed himself. His death turned him into a martyr for republican liberty and a hero for later generations, and Caesar''s African campaign was over.'),
  ('Battle of Munda', -45, 'On 17 March 45 BC Julius Caesar defeated the last Pompeian army at Munda in southern Spain, in the final battle of his civil war. It was a hard fight that Caesar is said to have called the only one where he fought for his life, and Titus Labienus and Gnaeus Pompeius, the elder son of Pompey, were killed. Caesar returned to Rome as master of the Roman world, but he was assassinated a year later.'),
  ('Julius Caesar assassinated', -44, 'On the Ides of March, 15 March 44 BC, Julius Caesar was stabbed about 23 times by a group of senators in the Theatre of Pompey in Rome, led by Brutus and Cassius. The conspirators claimed to be defending the Republic from a would-be king. Instead of restoring it, the murder started new civil wars that ended with Caesar''s heir Octavian ruling alone, and led to the Roman Empire.'),
  ('Battle of Philippi', -42, 'In two battles at Philippi in Macedonia in October 42 BC, the armies of Mark Antony and Octavian defeated those of Caesar''s assassins Brutus and Cassius, who both killed themselves. It was the last stand of the Republican cause. Antony and Octavian then divided the Roman world between them, until their own rivalry led to Actium.'),
  ('Battle of Actium', -31, 'On 2 September 31 BC Octavian''s fleet under Marcus Agrippa defeated the combined fleets of Mark Antony and Cleopatra off Actium, on the Ionian coast of Greece. Antony and Cleopatra escaped with part of their ships and fled to Egypt, where both died by suicide the next year. The victory made Octavian sole ruler of the Roman world, and within a few years he became Augustus, the first emperor.'),
  ('Roman annexation of Egypt', -30, 'In the summer of 30 BC Octavian invaded Egypt after the defeat of Antony and Cleopatra at Actium, and Alexandria fell as Antony''s remaining forces deserted. Antony and Cleopatra killed themselves, ending the Ptolemaic dynasty. Egypt became a Roman province ruled by a governor chosen by Octavian himself, and its grain fed the city of Rome.'),
  ('Augustus becomes first Roman emperor', -27, 'In 27 BC the Senate gave Octavian the title Augustus and wide powers, and historians count this as the start of the Roman Empire. Augustus presented his rule as a restoration of the Republic, but in practice he held the real power for the rest of his life. His reign began the Pax Romana, about two centuries of relative peace and prosperity across the Mediterranean world.'),
  ('Battle of Teutoburg Forest', 9, 'In September AD 9 the Germanic leader Arminius ambushed three Roman legions under Publius Quinctilius Varus in the forests of northern Germany and destroyed them. Some 15,000 to 20,000 Romans died, and Augustus is said to have cried out for Varus to give him back his legions. Rome gave up its plan to conquer Germany east of the Rhine, which stayed the empire''s frontier for centuries.'),
  ('Destruction of Jerusalem', 70, 'In AD 70 the Roman army under Titus, son of the emperor Vespasian, took Jerusalem after a siege of several months and burned the Second Temple. Much of the population was killed, enslaved or driven out, and the city was razed. The destruction ended the main phase of the First Jewish Revolt and marked a turning point in Jewish history, as the loss of the Temple pushed Judaism toward a religion centred on the synagogue and scripture.'),
  ('Siege of Masada', 73, 'In AD 72-73 Roman forces took the fortress of Masada, a cliff-top stronghold held by Jewish rebels, by building a huge siege ramp against its walls. According to Josephus, our only source, the defenders chose mass suicide rather than capture. Masada became a lasting symbol of Jewish resistance, though historians debate how much of the story is accurate.'),
  ('Battle of Mons Graupius', 83, 'In AD 83 or 84 the Roman governor Agricola defeated the Caledonians under Calgacus at Mons Graupius in what is now Scotland, according to his son-in-law, the historian Tacitus. Tacitus put a famous speech into Calgacus''s mouth, accusing Rome of making a desert and calling it peace. The site of the battle is unknown and Tacitus may have exaggerated, and the Romans soon pulled back from the Scottish Highlands.'),
  ('Battle of Guandu', 200, 'In 200 AD Cao Cao''s smaller army defeated the far larger force of Yuan Shao at Guandu, after a raid burned the enemy''s grain supplies. It was the turning point in the struggle for northern China. Cao Cao became its dominant power, laying the foundation for the later state of Cao Wei in the Three Kingdoms period.'),
  ('Battle of Red Cliffs', 208, 'In the winter of 208-209 AD the allied forces of Sun Quan and Liu Bei defeated the larger fleet of the northern warlord Cao Cao on the Yangtze River at Red Cliffs, partly by setting fire to his ships. The victory prevented Cao Cao from reuniting China and made the division into three kingdoms possible. It became one of the most famous battles in Chinese history, retold in the novel Romance of the Three Kingdoms.'),
  ('Battle of Nisibis', 217, 'In 217 AD the Roman emperor Macrinus fought a Parthian army under Artabanus IV for three days near Nisibis in northern Mesopotamia, and the Romans came off worse. Macrinus bought peace with a large payment and returned home, which his own soldiers saw as a humiliation. It was the last major battle between Rome and the Parthian Empire, which fell to the Sasanians within a decade.'),
  ('Sasanian Empire founded', 224, 'In 224 AD Ardashir I defeated the last Parthian king, Artabanus IV, and founded the Sasanian Empire, which ruled Persia until the Arab conquest in the 7th century. The Sasanians revived Persian traditions and made Zoroastrianism the state religion. For four centuries they were Rome''s and Byzantium''s main rival, and several times their kings defeated Roman emperors.'),
  ('Battle of Wuzhang Plains', 234, 'In 234 AD the Shu chancellor Zhuge Liang led his fifth and last Northern Expedition against Wei and faced the Wei commander Sima Yi on the Wuzhang Plains in a long stalemate. Zhuge Liang fell ill and died in camp, and the Shu army withdrew. His loyalty and ingenuity made him one of the most admired figures of the Three Kingdoms, and Shu''s offensives against Wei faded after his death.'),
  ('Battle of Edessa', 260, 'In 260 AD the Roman emperor Valerian was defeated and captured by the Persian king Shapur I near Edessa, together with a large part of his army. It was the first time a Roman emperor was taken prisoner by a foreign enemy, and Valerian died in captivity. Shapur had the triumph carved on rock reliefs in Persia, and the disaster deepened the Crisis of the Third Century.'),
  ('Jin reunification', 280, 'In 280 AD the Western Jin dynasty conquered Eastern Wu and reunited China, ending the Three Kingdoms period after nearly sixty years of division. The unity did not last: within a generation the empire was torn apart by the War of the Eight Princes and by invasions from the north. The Jin court fled south in 317, and China stayed divided for most of the next three centuries.'),
  ('Battle of Milvian Bridge', 312, 'On 28 October 312 Constantine defeated his rival Maxentius at the Milvian Bridge near Rome, and Maxentius drowned in the Tiber. According to Christian writers, Constantine had seen a heavenly sign before the battle and had his soldiers mark their shields with the Christian symbol. The victory made him master of the West, started his path to sole rule over the empire, and began the shift of the Roman Empire toward Christianity.'),
  ('Constantine''s conversion', 312, 'Before the Battle of the Milvian Bridge in 312, Constantine is said to have seen a vision of a cross of light, or a Christian sign in a dream, and to have been told that he would conquer by it. Historians still debate how Christian he really became then, since he was baptized only on his deathbed in 337. Whatever the truth, Constantine''s favour turned a persecuted sect into the dominant religion of the Roman Empire.'),
  ('Edict of Milan', 313, 'In February 313 the emperors Constantine and Licinius met in Milan and agreed to treat Christians kindly across the Roman Empire. The agreement gave Christians freedom of worship and returned property taken during persecutions. It ended the era of official persecution and was a major step toward the Christian Church''s later position of power, although it granted toleration to all religions rather than favouring one.'),
  ('Council of Nicaea', 325, 'The First Council of Nicaea, called by Emperor Constantine in AD 325, was the first great gathering of bishops from across the Christian world. It condemned the teaching of Arius that the Son was created and not eternal, and it produced the first form of the Nicene Creed. It also set rules for fixing the date of Easter, and it shows that the Roman emperor now acted as a patron and arbiter of the Church.'),
  ('Battle of Mursa Major', 351, 'On 28 September 351 the eastern Roman emperor Constantius II defeated the western usurper Magnentius at Mursa, in modern Croatia, in one of the bloodiest battles in Roman history. Both armies suffered huge losses, enough that Constantius''s victory has been called a Pyrrhic one. Many historians think the loss of so many trained soldiers weakened Rome''s ability to defend its frontiers in the following decades.'),
  ('Battle of Strasbourg', 357, 'In 357 the Caesar Julian, commanding a smaller Roman army, decisively defeated a large Alamannic confederation under King Chnodomar at Strasbourg, then called Argentoratum. Chnodomar was captured and the Alamanni were driven back across the Rhine. The victory secured the Rhine frontier of Gaul and made Julian so popular with his troops that they proclaimed him emperor in 360.'),
  ('Battle of Adrianople', 378, 'On 9 August 378 a Gothic army under Fritigern destroyed the Eastern Roman army of Emperor Valens at Adrianople, in modern Turkey, and Valens himself died in the battle. Roman infantry was overrun by the Gothic cavalry, which returned at a crucial moment. The defeat opened the Balkans to the Goths, who were allowed to settle inside the empire, and it is often seen as the beginning of the end for Rome in the West.'),
  ('Battle of Fei River', 383, 'In autumn 383 the much smaller army of the Eastern Jin dynasty defeated the huge army of the Former Qin ruler Fu Jian at the Fei River in China. Fu Jian had hoped to unify China, but his multi-ethnic empire fell apart after the defeat. It saved the south of China from conquest and let the Eastern Jin survive for another generation, and it gave Chinese the saying about mistaking every bush and tree for an enemy soldier.'),
  ('Battle of the Catalaunian Plains', 451, 'On 20 June 451 a coalition led by the Roman general Aetius and the Visigothic king Theodoric I fought Attila''s Huns and their allies on the Catalaunian Plains in Gaul. Theodoric was killed, but the Huns suffered heavy losses and withdrew. The battle stopped Attila''s advance into Gaul and was one of the last major military operations of the Western Roman Empire.');
